review_text:
An enormous universe that somehow feels tiny
No Man’s Sky has some great ideas. The Corvette is probably my favourite part: building it gradually, flying it, using autopilot, walking around the interior and landing a large ship on planets genuinely feels good.
Unfortunately, most of the game feels like a collection of disconnected systems rather than one coherent universe.
My biggest problem is the lack of scale and discovery. You enter a supposedly unknown system and fleets immediately warp in around you. You land on a new planet and ships are already constantly flying overhead. Almost every system becomes the same pattern: a few planets, a station, markers everywhere and familiar POIs. The galaxy is enormous, but rarely feels remote or mysterious.
The game also seems afraid to challenge the player. Instead of letting scarcity, uncertainty, distance or limitations create satisfaction, it constantly tries to make everything accessible and convenient. Almost everything is marked, teleportation makes distance meaningless, exploration rarely requires real investigation, and even fossils can be freely mixed into imaginary creatures instead of requiring you to reconstruct an actual species.
Then there are endless inconsistencies:
- your suit works in vacuum, but underwater you suddenly need separate oxygen;
- a ship cannot launch without fuel, but can still be magically summoned;
- small refiners need fuel while larger ones do not;
- storage needs electricity while more advanced devices often do not;
- Sentinels instantly switch from ground pursuit to space pursuit when you enter your ship;
- settlements simulate debt and productivity, but becoming profitable mostly rewards you with arbitrary items;
- quest targets repeatedly give intentionally approximate locations just to force the same scanner routine.
There are also only a few dominant alien civilizations, stations quickly become repetitive, and the same small set of planetary structures appears everywhere.
No Man’s Sky has enormous breadth, but very little systemic coherence or meaningful challenge.
The Corvette shows how good the game can be when a system has weight, progression and purpose. I just wish the rest of the universe felt less like Far Cry in space and more like actual exploration.

I realized I never did review this. Long ago, I bought PZ and refunded it, as I didn't find it fun and it barely ran on my laptop. Since then,a lot of stuff changed, mostly my taste in game and this game overall - I played unstable for a while before 42 became the stable and I must say two things;
When finished, this WILL be the definitive zombie survival game; not only does actual survival tactics here help, but common sense works (for the most part, how come I can boil bandages to sterilize them, but cant pry open a door with a crowbar in vanila?!) and zombies are a constant and real threat, even one mistake can cost you ALL;
"But that's not my taste!" Great! Sandbox it up, I usually play default apocalypse but lower generator fuel consumption as I dont want to babysit my safehouse - No wrong way to play. Turn off infection or make the zombies sprint; You always die, but how is the important question; did you go guns blazing or took an unnecessary risk? Did you end up in a high speed fatal collision or ate a raw egg with bleach? Did you forget the stove on?
Regardless, the PROS - high customization of your experience, fun and unforgiving game for some, action PDF sheet for others. GREAT modding,integrated with the workshop and a passionate community both for MP and SP.

As for my cons. This will be a great game, but the development, while active, is slow. This is more so a buyer beware; it will be at least a century more imo before their rough roadmap is done. Regardless, that is that and I wish there were more sandbox options a-la survival journal mod so that if you die you can still kinda continue a save, not just be like "eh f- it" and hard reset; Notably, the longer a run lasts the less I care if it ends, weirdly, as it feels "right" but damn, does it suck when your lil zomboid duud decides to jump a window and die because you held E wrong. Also, performance can vary, so use settings accordingly :D

I have played this game off and off for years. This game Allows me to do everything I want whenever I want. I wanna run around as a robot fishy eating other fishy i can. I wanna spend hours Fishing normally I can. Wanna mine? Go explode rocks. You can play the entire game solo. I mainly have to because I have no friends.. ;.; 

My favorite frame is titania a pretty butterfly lady. The game monetization is really fair. Everything can be farmed and earned without plat. Plat itself is easy to farm if you don't play alot of warframes.

Overall Game gud will spend another 1,000 hours on game.

No Man's Sky is a very simple and potent game. 

It can be jank, it can be annoying, but it will forever hold a very special place in my heart because it gave me infuriating creature scanning collecting and battling, and it told a very simple story that touched my cynical angry heart.

//Life isn't a question and it doesn't need an answer.//

Liked:
- different possible situations;
- pawn management;
- pawn possible effects and activities;
- lots of mod possibilities;
- base management.
Disliked:
- probably nothing in particular.

I go into the lab just for the boss to SEEMINGLY teleport me into the lava below;
Fire burns on water and I die after going in the water.

56 hours and I still die by the most RANDOM things possible. Oh and I still can't consistently get past the first biome :steamhappy:

a terrible game made not for PC but more for mobile phones, would make a lot of sense if u could speed up crafting and buy resources for donations. its not survival, its grinding simulator, there is no storyline, no interesting mechanics (except blood mechanic). in the whole game just a few bosses are cool and unique, others just recolored with pretty same abilities that are sometimes just dishonest. you are also attached to your lvl, you have no freedom in your progression, new armor or new weapon gives you +x LVLs and you cannot upgrade any specific skill or ability, you are just chained on your level because devs decided you are supposed to. boss damage is just ridiculous, if u are underleveled for 1-2 levels, you deal 17 dmg and boss deals you 150 with one hit. If this game is not ragebait, I do not know what this is. camera and movement are also trash, holding RMB to move camera is NOT convenient, for your information. 

If you do not have a job or you want to be a resource miner and spend time heading to the mine forth and back for a couple hours, then melt all of this and craft a piece of tunic that you have to upgrade in 10 mins after you slay the next boss= play it. If not, find anything else but this game
]

